#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554680} :activity/id #uuid "b63b672a-110b-5025-89a1-a1f474d71b68" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049265 :topic/id #uuid "e80c9bca-b203-5866-835e-d0d29bbfdf22" :topic/math-academy-id 1995} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554602 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00514e84-3144-5df4-82c2-33eb39ce62b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553865} {:answer/id #uuid "8db89be6-e547-5fa5-9888-b55fe82c1cef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}(4-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554170} {:answer/id #uuid "9273e530-04e8-5996-b68a-1f42eb0f608e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}(2-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554192} {:answer/id #uuid "e88ae27f-5535-584b-9730-b1ae58287e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}(8-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554372} {:answer/id #uuid "e892a90b-d1ba-521a-b1de-129de62c0978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}(4-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554373}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554373} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5e8aa5a-ac76-5fe7-8cbb-d1e2111d6174" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8fe28743-a486-54a9-8fdd-bcbf858bcbbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-139035" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2{e}^{t/2},\\,y={e}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t/2})={e}^{t/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{t/2})}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{t/2}\\cdot {e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{t}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}({4}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}(8-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}(4-\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1aa63779-111f-54e9-892c-0fb614322783" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554693}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2e8bc30-41c9-56c8-a5b2-e047a033dedb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{-t}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367465} {:answer/id #uuid "b854c123-c85d-5e04-a948-bd8a101d0416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{-t}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367466} {:answer/id #uuid "14edfcb2-c8de-5378-b4d9-078fcd77b9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367467} {:answer/id #uuid "8922920d-3e40-5b79-b054-c8ad34ddd743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367468} {:answer/id #uuid "f50415c4-c627-581d-942c-cb7597564764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367469}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367467} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c091cf5-1213-5f71-95d4-647a3b748c8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1cc47049-1d82-526c-84a3-b9c51fd53a09" :question/math-academy-id "q-139026" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2{e}^{t},\\,y=2{e}^{-t},\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=-2$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})=2{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{-t})=-2{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}2{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{(2{e}^{t})}^{2}+{(-2{e}^{-t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}+4{e}^{-2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{4({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of the hyperbolic cosine $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t=\\frac{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}{2},$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =8\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "67d2476e-8b28-5c63-84c6-efc8f7cef93c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554653}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554693 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34815984-fefd-5798-bdd6-d8ac9e63495b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266603} {:answer/id #uuid "5f3baba4-8572-53a6-81aa-d3e89b039d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{3{t}^{4}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266984} {:answer/id #uuid "6f44b9c5-062e-5836-8fa5-9b241e49df01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267122} {:answer/id #uuid "9a5f9d3b-c7f5-5304-ad78-7cc3982df96e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267539} {:answer/id #uuid "b26a0858-4124-5ef1-b554-c41d2275431e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267740}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266603} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab978d17-dd39-5ce8-a339-813c29c490be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3645ded7-1477-514e-b282-7668816fc19c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139056" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{3},\\,y=2t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "83dd259c-644a-5187-abca-3fbeaba6c996" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554697}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3256b67-7e99-5826-a95a-b55a197e4d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530184} :answer-field/id #uuid "12fa28de-d131-50b4-b0e1-7dd696b0e54f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4f72f786-eb31-5b03-9051-ae70858e9f5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-352035" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{4},\\,t\\in [0,\\sqrt[4]{6}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\sqrt[4]{6}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{4})=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}(1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\cdot 2|t|\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t⟹{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{3}{16}\\text{d}u.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt[4]{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{3}{16}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[(9)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(27-1) \\\\  & =13\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "86a8d041-feed-5f21-a7aa-0238cf6d1240" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554707}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c15a0040-721e-5583-80c4-6b4b6d161036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi(2-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530062}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530062} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b596d95-5abf-5a25-8619-bd46ab687c7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "95891d65-20e2-5732-9bd4-e52bbf49b5d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-352088" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t/2)}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{csc}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-2+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-2+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cot}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1}2\\pi \\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {[u]}_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi (1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9479a2f7-46a4-5db5-a1e5-a589077eded2"}] :activity/title "Surface Areas of Revolution for Parametric Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553855}]}
